=== Doeg · Psalm 52
--- David is come to the house of Ahimelech
kicker: Psalm 52 · Doeg
p: Psalm 52 is told to us as a Psalm written when Doeg the Edomite came and told Saul, and said unto him, David is come to the house of Ahimelech.
p: The situation is described in 1 Samuel 21, David has fled Saul, who is determined to kill him. Consumed with pride and evil spirits, the king seeks the life of a man considered a hero among Israel.

--- The shew bread and Goliath's sword
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Nob
p: David seeks food and weapons at the Tabernacle in Nob, from the unsuspecting priest, Ahimelech. Ahimelech unwittingly provides David with the shew bread, and Goliath’s sword as David claims he is on a secret mission for the King.
big: 1 Samuel 21:2

--- Have ye not read what David did
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Nob
p: (An act that Jesus uses to justify His Apostles plucking corn to eat on the Sabbath.)
scripture: Matthew 12:3-4

--- Doeg, an Edomite
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Nob
p: But the priest isn’t the only person there to see David.
big: 1 Samuel 21:7 | mark: Doeg, an Edomite
p: Now, Doeg is not a man of war. He is a herdsman. He oversees the other shepherds and wranglers for the King. But he took the opportunity of seeing David there in Nob, on the run, to curry favor with Saul.

--- A captain over them
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: The story continues, showing David’s flight, ending up in Judah at the advice of the prophet Gad, bringing his parents to the King of Moab for protection, and gathering with him loyal men as he went.
big: 1 Samuel 22:2
p: (This is a lovely picture of Jesus and salvation, the distressed and debtors gathering unto him.)

--- Hear now, ye Benjamites
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: Saul hears tell of these activities;
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:6-7

--- Doeg enters the picture
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: As Saul assembles his armies, Doeg enters the picture;
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:9-10 | mark: Then answered Doeg the Edomite
p: We should note that much time has passed since David’s flight to Nob, and Saul has an idea of where David and his men have hidden, in the forest of Hareth, in Judah.

--- Why have ye conspired against me?
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: But Doeg brings his knowledge to the King as he is already readying for war. It feels even more opportunistic and craven in light of that passage of time and the context. Doeg’s word causes Saul to summon the priest and his kin;
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:11-13

--- Thou shalt surely die, Ahimelech
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: The hapless Ahimelech then pleads ignorance and asks that Saul understand that he had no way of knowing David fled the King.
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:15-16

--- Slay the priests
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: Saul refuses clemency. In his murderous rage he orders the death of the priest and entire lineage.
big: 1 Samuel 22:17
p: Even Saul’s loyal men hesitate and refuse the order of their King, out of fear of the Lord and harming His anointed priests. In the state the King was in, in the context of his twisted, murderous vengeance, the footmen feared God more.

--- But not Doeg the Edomite
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: But not Doeg the Edomite.
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:18-19 | mark: And Doeg the Edomite turned

--- Doeg the herdsman
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: Doeg the shepherd, Doeg the herdsman, who brought out of date intelligence to Saul’s attention, jeopardizing the priest, that led to this situation, took full advantage of the license given him by Saul, and wickedly carried out the command. First slaying 85 priests, and then murdering every man, woman, and child, beast and all, found in the Levite city of Nob.

--- Abiathar escaped
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: One of the sons of Ahimelech escapes to see David, telling him of the wicked act done at the hands of Doeg;
scripture: 1 Samuel 22:20-23

--- Abide thou with me
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: David’s guilt at not taking care of Doeg when he had the opportunity, and the resulting deaths, has him offer Abiathar refuge with him and his troop of men.
p: David will have Abiathar act in his office, with the ephod, to consult with the Lord several times. Asking Will the men of Keilah deliver me up into his hand? will Saul come down, as thy servant hath heard? [[1 Samuel 23:11]] And later, enquiring of the Lord, Shall I pursue after this troop? shall I overtake them? [[1 Samuel 30:8]] Seeking the wisdom of God to direct his paths in times of uncertainty.

--- God has abandoned Saul
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: David, in exile, is given the oracles of God through the Levite and ephod, as a King would. Saul, in his rage had cut himself off from this avenue of prophecy. As the sole survivor of the high priestly line of Eli, Abiathar brings the official religious authority of Israel to David’s camp, signaling that God has abandoned Saul.
p: By using the Urim and Thummim in the ephod, Abiathar provides divine reconnaissance that saves David from Saul’s traps and ensures the defeat of the Amalekites. Abiathar serves as David’s high priest throughout his exile and reign, eventually helping bring the Ark of the Covenant to Jerusalem.

--- Evil became good for David
kicker: 1 Samuel 22 · Doeg
p: During Absalom’s rebellion, Abiathar remains behind in Jerusalem to act as a secret agent, feeding David crucial intelligence through his son Jonathan to help win back the throne.
p: The evil of Saul and the bloodthirst of Doeg, became good for David, who loved God and was called according to His purpose.

--- O mighty man
kicker: Psalm 52 · Doeg
p: But Doeg became a byword.
p: Psalm 52 mockingly called Doeg a "mighty man," for slaying men of God, dressed only in linen, and helpless women and children, even babies. Doeg did what was unthinkable to the actual mighty men of Saul, after causing the entire situation by his obsequious talebearing. Immortalized in his wickedness by the psalmist, David.
scripture: Psalm 52:1-3 | mark: O mighty man

--- O thou deceitful tongue
kicker: Psalm 52 · Doeg
big: Psalm 52:4

--- Doeg's fate
kicker: Psalm 52 · Doeg
p: We can imagine Doeg’s fate, the Bible doesn’t speak specifically of what became of him afterwards, but Psalm 52 gives us some idea of his ultimate fate before God.
scripture: Psalm 52:5-7

--- By his evil, good came
kicker: Psalm 52 · Doeg
scripture: Psalm 52:8-9 | mark: I trust in the mercy of God for ever and ever
p: But by his evil, good came. David trusted in the mercy of the Lord, and was strengthened by Doeg’s actions, and was able to sing of the praises of the Lord because of it.

=== The deceitful tongue · Psalm 52
--- Psalm 52 · The mighty man [title]
heading: Psalm 52 · The mighty man

--- The polar opposite of Psalm 51
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:1 | mark: O mighty man
p: In **Psalm 52**, the psalmist presents the polar opposite of the contrite heart of **Psalm 51**, the mischief-making man who doesn't avail himself to the longsuffering mercy of God.

--- The deceitful tongue
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
scripture: Psalm 52:2-4 | mark: O thou deceitful tongue
p: The wicked and their deceitful tongue, inventing lies, telling slander against God and God's people, boasting of their blessings, not thankful but vain in their imagination, loving evil more than good, these are the men who continually reject the call to fear the Lord.

--- Like just Lot
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: Those of us who are saved can become incredulous at the prideful stance of the wicked, who reject the fear of God and lean on their own strength. We can often grow confounded and even depressed over the ways of the wicked. Like just Lot, living in Sodom, being afflicted by the wickedness around him
big: 2 Peter 2:8

--- The Bible is not afraid to call these people out
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: The Bible is not afraid to call these people out, directly and to their face. They are boastful, mockingly called "mighty men," liars, evil, haters of righteousness, lovers of slanderous and wicked words, lovers of mischief, having deceitful tongues.
p: We should not fear to identify exactly the perverted in our midst when they reject and mock the gospel of God and the word we preach.

--- Pulling them out of the fire
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: Nor should we tiptoe around the heart of the matter; Their imminent destruction and the torment in the fire of Hell that awaits them.
scripture: Jude 1:22-23

--- Hard preaching
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: Those who do not see the wrath of God revealed from Heaven upon others as a reason to repent and believe, and won't see the mercy and hear the praise of the meek, feeling the compassionate love of believers magnify God's grace, need hard preaching to let them know exactly what they've chosen, in hopes that they might be saved.
p: That their love of devouring words will lead to God likewise destroying them forever. Literally, being devoured by flame and worm in outer darkness, forever. Eternal suffering at the hands of a just and mighty God.

--- Selah
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:5
p: If "selah" is meant to be a pause, a moment to reflect on what was just sung or said, is there anywhere more fitting than in that moment, having heard that sobering truth?
p: God will destroy you. He will take your life and root you out of the land of the living, to place you in a place of eternal destruction.

--- Whoso diggeth a pit
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:6
p: And the righteous shall see their end and fear God, and laugh at the calamity they brought about on themselves. The end their mischief brought about, turned back upon them.
big: Proverbs 26:27

--- A parable or a byword
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: The ironic nature of the bitter end experienced by the boastful sinner is often evident and clear to those that fear God. The humor of the Lord is appreciated by His children. We laugh at their calamity.
p: When the wicked are made a parable or a byword among the people, it should give other men pause. The wrath revealed from Heaven should give instruction to the simple to turn towards God. It shows the righteous in Christ God's power and promise.
big: Proverbs 1:7

--- God will not be mocked
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
p: The warning is ignored by the fool. And God will not be mocked.
scripture: Proverbs 1:25-27

--- God will not be mocked, continued
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
scripture: Proverbs 1:28-30

--- Their own puny strength
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:7
p: These men that did not trust in God, but made their own puny strength and their worldly, perishing riches their stronghold, will be brought low. By their evil deeds they seek to maintain power and control, and by the same they will be brought low by our God, Who is a consuming fire.

--- But we are His
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:8 | mark: I trust in the mercy of God for ever and ever
p: But we are His. We have humbled ourselves to rely on His holy name. To be meek servants, poor in spirit, that we may receive His grace, and the abundance of life in His love.

--- We will praise Him forever
kicker: Psalm 52 · The mighty man
big: Psalm 52:9
p: They waited for Jesus, we look back to His cross and the glory of His resurrection. We shall praise His name among the people. We shall look at His mercy and wrath, and know He is our salvation. We will praise Him forever, as His saints. Amen

